{{Quote|I am Daughter&hellip;|Morai|<ref name="A World Between Worlds" />}}
'''Morai''' was a [[Gender|female]] [[convor]] with spiritual ties to the [[Daughter]], a [[Force wielder]] from [[Mortis]]. She appeared in various depictions of the Force wielders alongside the Daughter. It was believed by some that the convor in these depictions was a servant of the Daughter or even a manifestation of the Daughter herself in another form. Morai became a friend of former [[Jedi]] [[Padawan]] [[Ahsoka Tano]], whom the Daughter transferred her life essence into prior to her [[death]], and came to accompany and watch over her. Tano claimed that she owed the convor her life.

==Biography==
{{Quote|Morai?''"<br />"''She's an old friend. I owe her my life&hellip;|Ezra Bridger and Ahsoka Tano|<ref name="A World Between Worlds" />}}
[[File:Gist-CW15Years-AhsokaTano.png|thumb|left|250px|Morai was a special convor with spiritual ties to the Daughter and a connection to Ahsoka Tano.]]
Morai was a [[Gender|female]] [[convor]] known to have spiritual ties to the [[Daughter]],<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> one of the [[Force wielder]]s of [[Mortis]].<ref name="Daughter">{{Databank|daughter|Daughter}}</ref> She was shown alongside the Daughter in various depictions of them, such as the [[painting of the Mortis gods]] in the [[Lothal Jedi Temple]]. Some believed that Morai was a servant of the Daughter, or even a manifestation of the Daughter herself in another form.<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> In [[20 BBY]],<ref name="SWGA">''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]''</ref> the [[Jedi]] [[Anakin Skywalker]], [[Obi-Wan Kenobi]], and [[Ahsoka Tano]] [[Mission to Mortis|visited]] Mortis, as the [[Father (Mortis)|Father]] had wanted to test whether Skywalker was the [[Chosen One]] of prophecy.<ref name="Overlords">{{TCW|Overlords}}</ref> During the events that followed, Tano was killed by the [[Son]], who later stabbed his sister with the [[Dagger of Mortis]] when she jumped in front of it to protect her father. Dying, the Daughter sacrificed her remaining life force to revive Tano.<ref name="Altar of Mortis">{{TCW|Altar of Mortis (episode)}}</ref> The Father later killed himself and the Son before the three Jedi were returned to the normal universe.<ref name="Ghosts of Mortis">{{TCW|Ghosts of Mortis}}</ref>

In time after the life force transferal,<ref name="Altar of Mortis" /> Morai became a friend of Tano, coming to watch over her. According to Tano, she owed Morai her life.<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> A few [[Standard year|years]] after the [[Proclamation of the New Order|rise]] of the [[Galactic Empire]],<ref name="VAD EG">{{SW|url=series/clone-wars/victory-and-death-episode-guide|text="Victory and Death" Episode Guide - The Clone Wars}}</ref> Skywalker, now the [[Sith Lord]] [[Darth]] Vader, visited the [[Unidentified moon|moon]] where the [[Venator-class Star Destroyer|''Venator''-class Star Destroyer]]<ref name="Victory and Death">{{TCW|Victory and Death}}</ref> ''[[Tribunal]]''<ref name="Join the Battle" /> had crashed during [[Order 66]], as it had been carrying Tano and [[Rex]] at the time, although they had [[Skirmish aboard the Tribunal|escaped]]. Vader found the abandoned [[Ahsoka Tano's lightsabers|lightsaber]] of his former apprentice in the wreckage, and looked up to see Morai circling above him in the sky before he left the scene.<ref name="Victory and Death" />

[[File:A-World-Between-Worlds.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Morai appears in the World Between Worlds]]
During the establishment of [[Chopper Base]] on [[Atollon]], Morai was present and watched the conflict between the [[Spectres|crew]] of the ''[[Ghost]]'' and the [[krykna]]. Later, [[Ezra Bridger]] watched Morai fly overhead before Tano arrived so she, Bridger, and [[Kanan Jarrus]] could [[Mission to Malachor|travel]] to [[Malachor]].<ref name="TMOCB">{{Rebels|The Mystery of Chopper Base}}</ref> When Bridger [[Mission to the Lothal Jedi Temple|entered]] the [[World Between Worlds]], Morai was present and encouraged him to save Tano by pulling her out of a [[portal]]. After reuniting with Tano, she was present when Tano and Bridger fought against [[Galactic Emperor|Emperor]] [[Darth Sidious|Palpatine]]. Morai accompanied Tano when she returned through the portal to the ruined [[Malachor Sith Temple]].<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> As Tano returned to the [[Sith temple|temple]] interior, Morai overlooked an injured Darth Vader as he limped away from the scene before flying down to join Tano in the trek into the Sith temple.<ref name="Twilight of the Apprentice">{{Rebels|Twilight of the Apprentice}}</ref>

Morai reached a flooded area in the depths of the temple before Tano and waited for her. Once Tano arrived,<ref name="Artist series 3">{{SWCT|set=Artist Series - Dave Filoni|cardname=3 (Ahsoka Tano)}}</ref> Morai flew above the water that Tano trekked through and guided her<ref name="Artist series 7">{{SWCT|set=Artist Series - Dave Filoni|cardname=7 (Ahsoka Tano)}}</ref> to a stairway,<ref name="Artist series 8">{{SWCT|set=Artist Series - Dave Filoni|cardname=8 (Ahsoka Tano)}}</ref> which led to another portal into the World Between Worlds.<ref name="Artist series 10">{{SWCT|set=Artist Series - Dave Filoni|cardname=10 (Ahsoka Tano)}}</ref> Morai then departed the temple and was seen by Vader as she made her way to the open skies of Malachor.<ref name="Artist series 9">{{SWCT|set=Artist Series - Dave Filoni|cardname=9 (Ahsoka Tano)}}</ref> Morai was later present for the meeting of the [[Mandalorian]] [[Din Djarin]] and Tano<ref name="Chapter 13">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 13: The Jedi}}</ref> in [[9 ABY]]<ref name="Date">{{MandoDate}}</ref> on the planet [[Corvus]].<ref name="Chapter 13" />

Following Tano's [[Quest to find Ezra Bridger|mission]] to the [[Far galaxy|extragalactic]] world of [[Peridea]] in the hunt for the missing [[Grand Admiral]] [[Mitth'raw'nuruodo|Thrawn]] and [[Bokken Jedi]] [[Ezra Bridger]], the [[Battle of Peridea|result]] of which left her and her apprentice [[Sabine Wren]] stranded there, Morai again appeared to her. Perched on a rock near a village of [[Noti]], she looked on as Tano gave her a contemplative look, before flying off.<ref name="Ahsoka 8">{{Ahsoka|Part Eight: The Jedi, the Witch, and the Warlord}}</ref>

==Personality and traits==
Morai was a female convor with [[Color|green]] and white feathers and green [[eye]]s. Associated with the Daughter, Morai was attuned to the [[light side of the Force]].<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> She was 0.2 [[meter]]s (8 inches) tall. After the Daughter sacrificed her essence to save Tano, Morai began to appear in Tano's presence, which led to some questioning if the Daughter was entirely gone. Morai acted as an observer, watching over Tano and lingering in places important to her.<ref name="Join the Battle" />

==Ability==
As Morai was associated with the Daughter, she too was known for her [[Force-sensitive|Force-sensitivity]].  She notably had the ability to appear literally anywhere regardless of where Ahsoka was, including the [[Extragalactic]] World of Peridea, and even the [[World Between Worlds]].<ref name="Join the Battle" /> 

==Behind the scenes==
Morai first appeared in the ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]'' [[Star Wars Rebels Season Two|season two]] episode "[[The Mystery of Chopper Base]],"<ref name="TMOCB" /> and was first identified in the [[Star Wars Rebels Season Four|fourth season]] episode "[[A World Between Worlds]]."<ref name="A World Between Worlds" /> In [[wikipedia:Greek Mythology|Greek Mythology]], the similarly spelled [[wikipedia:Moirai|Moirai]], known in English as the Fates, were goddesses who were the determiners and personifications of fate and destiny.<ref name="Britannica">{{WebCite|url=https://www.britannica.com/topic/Fate-Greek-and-Roman-mythology|text=Fate|quote='''Fate''', Greek '''Moira''', plural '''Moirai''', Latin '''Parca''', plural '''Parcae''', in Greek and Roman mythology, any of three goddesses who determined human destinies, and in particular the span of a person's life and his allotment of misery and suffering.|archivedate=20230609102218}}</ref>

According to ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' supervising director [[Dave Filoni]], Morai's appearance during the final moments of "[[Victory and Death]]" went through different versions, including one in which Morai perched on the [[BTL-B Y-wing starfighter/bomber]]. However, besides having to take into consideration how much time and budget he had for the episode, Filoni felt that the scene was not playing right, which led to the final version of the convor's appearance.<ref name="Dave Filoni's Different Versions">{{OfficialYouTube|channel=channel/UCTAgbu2l6_rBKdbTvEodEDw|name=Nerdist|video=0Wb8nSPmRr0|text=Dave Filoni Breaks Down Anakin and Ahsoka's Final Meeting in The Clone Wars! (Nerdist News Edition)}}</ref>

